<v Ira Glass>她离开德克萨斯好几个月。

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:28.390
<v Ira Glass>But a college freshman named Katie told us that the biggest aftereffect of her abortion and the new laws was that it led to a lot of awkward conversations with family and friends because she didn't want to tell everybody about her abortion. She wanted to be one of the millions of people who have had abortions and were able to put it away, move on, not think about it again.
<v Ira Glass>但一位名叫凯蒂的大一新生告诉我们，堕胎和新法律对她最大的后遗症是导致她与家人和朋友之间产生了许多尴尬的对话，因为她不想告诉所有人她堕胎的事。她想成为数百万堕胎后能够放下、继续前行、不再去想它的人之一。

00:47:28.390 --> 00:47:42.970
<v Ira Glass>This was not the year for that. Abortion was constantly in the news. People were talking about it all the time. And Katie's roommate talked about how annoying the anti-abortion protesters were in the quad. Katie wanted to agree but without disclosing too much about her own abortion.
<v Ira Glass>今年情况并非如此。堕胎一直出现在新闻中。人们一直都在谈论它。凯蒂的室友谈到反堕胎抗议者在校园广场上有多烦人。凯蒂想表示赞同，却不能透露太多关于她自己堕胎的细节。

00:47:42.970 --> 00:48:01.850
<v Katie>And I told her that I had a friend that had to go to Louisiana and Washington and all this stuff because of Roe v. Wade got overturned. And if Texas didn't have all these laws, it would have been so much easier on her. But she had to wait so long just because of Roe v. Wade.
<v Katie>我告诉她，我有个朋友因为“罗案”被推翻而不得不去路易斯安那州和华盛顿州等地。如果德克萨斯州没有这些法律，她本来会轻松得多。但她因为“罗案”不得不等了那么久。

00:48:01.850 --> 00:48:35.400
<v Ira Glass>For Ashley, the new laws dominated a lot of her last year. She was in the unusual situation that under the abortion ban, she left the state to get an abortion, and then, months later, she discovered she was pregnant again. Everything about how she felt about this new pregnancy was shaped by that first post Roe abortion. She'd had her mom watch her three kids when she went out of state but lied to her about where she was going and what she was doing, and she'd turned to an abortion fund to help pay for it last time around. Now, pregnant again and thinking about a second abortion--
<v Ira Glass>对于阿什莉来说，新法律影响了她过去一年的生活。她的情况很特殊：在堕胎禁令下，她离州去做了堕胎，几个月后，她发现自己又怀孕了。她对这次新怀孕的所有感受都被第一次“罗案”后的堕胎经历所塑造。她曾让妈妈照顾三个孩子自己离州，但对妈妈撒谎说自己去了哪里、做了什么；上次她还求助于堕胎基金来支付费用。现在，又怀孕了，考虑第二次堕胎——

00:48:35.400 --> 00:49:18.020
<v Ashley>I felt selfish. I felt like I was taking the fund resources away from the 17-year-old who needed it way more than me. I just felt horribly selfish, and I didn't feel like it was a situation that I could put myself and my whole family through again. I can't travel another 1,800 miles and lie to my family and my kids about where Mommy is when I have a spot in my van. And we have a happy home. And we have space and love. So basically, I didn't feel like I could do it again and still feel good about it.
<v Ashley>我觉得很自私。我觉得自己是在从那个比我更需要资源的人手里抢钱，比如那个17岁的女孩。我只是觉得自己无比自私，而且我觉得这对我自己和整个家庭来说，并不是我能再次经历的情况。我不能再跨越1800英里，对我的家人和孩子撒谎说妈妈在哪里，而我的面包车里还有空间。我们有一个幸福的家。我们有空间和爱。所以基本上，我觉得我没法再做一次，并且还能问心无愧。

00:49:18.020 --> 00:49:29.360
<v Ira Glass>So she was keeping the pregnancy, even though her reasons for the first abortion still stood. She still had three kids on Medicaid. Her husband still didn't make enough money. And she still did not want to be pregnant.
<v Ira Glass>所以她决定留下孩子，尽管她第一次堕胎的理由依然存在。她依然有三个孩子靠医疗补助（Medicaid）生活。她丈夫赚的钱依然不够多。而且她依然不想怀孕。

00:49:29.360 --> 00:49:57.900
<v Ashley>Then it made me start to realize how many mothers of multiple children have to just pile another kid in there when they really didn't want to. Sometimes it's people that already have a minivan and have the space and have the love, which makes us feel even worse. But just because we have the space and we have the love doesn't mean we wanted to do that.
<v Ashley>这件事让我开始意识到，有多少养育着多个孩子的母亲，在并不想要的情况下只能再增加一个孩子。有时这些人已经有了面包车，有空间，有爱，这让我们感觉更糟。但仅仅因为我们有空间、有爱，并不代表我们想要那样做。

00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:26.650
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to the most consequential result of the new laws. The first group of post Roe babies, babies who never would have been born had the Supreme Court had not acted, are now coming into the world. One of those babies is Taylor's. You heard Taylor earlier, the Reddit nerd in Atlanta who flew to Florida for an abortion, but in the end, didn't go to her appointment. Caroline has been tracking what's happened since then, and that's where we'll end today's show.
<v Ira Glass>这带出了新法律最深远的后果。第一批“罗案”后的婴儿——那些如果最高法院没有采取行动本不会出生的孩子——正在来到这个世界上。其中一个孩子是泰勒的。你之前听到了泰勒的故事，那个在亚特兰大、飞往佛罗里达堕胎，但最终没有去诊所的Reddit用户。卡罗琳一直在跟踪之后发生的事，这就是我们今天节目结束的地方。

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:49.500
<v Caroline Kitchener>By the time Taylor entered her third trimester, she was very alone. It happened week by week. When she was 13 weeks pregnant, coming home to Atlanta, Taylor's mom and sister wanted to celebrate. They asked, can we rub your belly? Can we throw you a baby shower? No and no. Taylor was not interested.
<v Caroline Kitchener>当泰勒进入孕晚期时，她非常孤独。事情是一周一周发生的。当她怀孕13周回到亚特兰大时，泰勒的妈妈和姐妹想庆祝。她们问：我们可以摸摸你的肚子吗？我们可以给你办产前派对吗？不行，不行。泰勒毫无兴趣。

00:50:49.500 --> 00:51:05.010
<v Taylor>I didn't want people to get me things I didn't want. I didn't want my family to feel responsible for something that I really didn't plan to do. If I was married or something, I'd have a baby shower. But this is not the same type of situation. So I didn't want to ask anyone for anything.
<v Taylor>我不想要别人送我不想要的东西。我不想要家人对一件我真的没打算做的事感到责任。如果我结婚了之类的，我会办派对。但现在情况不同。所以我不想向任何人索取任何东西。

00:51:05.010 --> 00:51:24.890
<v Caroline Kitchener>It was almost like she had decided this was her thing. She got pregnant without planning to, so she was going to deal with it all by herself. Taylor's family was offended, which made Taylor want to pull away even more. By the time she was around 16 weeks pregnant, Taylor and her mom were no longer speaking.
<v Caroline Kitchener>就像她决定了这是她自己的事。她意外怀孕，所以她打算独自处理这一切。泰勒的家人感到被冒犯，这让泰勒更想退缩。当她怀孕大约16周时，泰勒和妈妈不再说话了。

00:51:24.890 --> 00:51:34.710
<v Caroline Kitchener>18 weeks, Taylor and her boyfriend were fighting. She wanted him to move back to Atlanta. He wanted her to move to Florida. They broke up.
<v Caroline Kitchener>18周，泰勒和男友吵架了。她想让他搬回亚特兰大。他想让她搬去佛罗里达。他们分手了。

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:57.150
<v Caroline Kitchener>At 22 weeks, Taylor was trying to take control of the situation. She would buy all the right baby things, line them up in all the right spots in her house, a mini fridge by the bed to store breast milk, small stacks of diapers in every room, so she could reach every little thing she needed when it was just her and the baby. This way, she thought, she could do this alone.
<v Caroline Kitchener>22周，泰勒试图掌控局面。她购买了所有合适的婴儿用品，把它们摆在家里合适的位置：床边放个迷你冰箱储存母乳，房间里堆着成叠的尿布，这样她就能在只有她和孩子时拿到所需的一切。她想，这样她就能独自应付了。

00:51:57.150 --> 00:52:30.630
<v Caroline Kitchener>At 24 weeks, Taylor lost her job. She needed to pass a test to keep it. And with everything that was happening with the baby, she said she just couldn't focus. 25 weeks, Taylor asked her ex, what role do you want with this baby? He said, I want to be involved. But there's wanting to be involved and being able to. She took that to mean he's out.
<v Caroline Kitchener>24周，泰勒丢了工作。她得通过考试才能保住职位。但随着孩子的一切发生，她说她根本无法集中精神。25周，泰勒问前任：你想要在这个孩子生活中扮演什么角色？他说：我想参与。但“想参与”和“能够参与”是两回事。她认为他的意思是彻底退出。

00:52:30.630 --> 00:52:50.840
<v Caroline Kitchener>So as she entered her third trimester, Taylor started imagining herself alone in the delivery room. She would Uber herself to the hospital. She scheduled a C-section. Four days before the date, I caught Taylor running around, getting ready. At that point, she said she didn't want anybody with her, especially her family.
<v Caroline Kitchener>所以当她进入孕晚期时，泰勒开始想象自己独自待在产房里。她会打Uber去医院。她安排了剖腹产。在日期前四天，我看到泰勒在到处忙碌。在那一刻，她说她不想让任何人陪她，尤其是家人。

00:52:50.840 --> 00:53:09.760
<v Taylor>Yeah, because I don't want everybody crowding me right after the birth, and then I don't want everybody in my house at the same time. And these are going to be my first moments learning how to be a mom. And I don't want to do that in front of anybody either.
<v Taylor>是的，因为我不想在孩子出生后所有人都围着我，我也不想所有人都同时挤在我的房子里。这些将是我学习当妈妈的第一刻。我也不想在任何人面前做这些。

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:19.570
<v Caroline Kitchener>On March 24, Taylor gave birth to a baby boy, exactly, to the day, nine months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.
<v Caroline Kitchener>3月24日，泰勒生下了一个男婴，刚好是在最高法院推翻“罗诉韦德案”九个月整的那一天。

00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:22.990
<v Taylor>It went so freaking well.
<v Taylor>一切进展得太顺利了。

00:53:22.990 --> 00:53:25.450
<v Caroline Kitchener>I called her when she got home from the hospital.
<v Caroline Kitchener>她在出院后我打电话给她。

00:53:25.450 --> 00:53:41.160
<v Taylor>Let me tell you something. The weirdest part about it is how fast it was. It was like 10 minutes that they pulled this kid out of me. I felt pressure. And then the next thing you know, there's a [BLEEP] kid. It was crazy.
<v Taylor>我告诉你一件事。最奇怪的部分是速度太快了。他们把孩子从我肚子里拉出来好像只用了10分钟。我感觉到了压力。然后下一秒，就多出了一个[哔]孩子。真是疯了。

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:58.120
<v Taylor>I don't think there will ever be anything that will measure up to how surreal it felt to see a child come out of your stomach. It's just like, whoa, I created that. You're just in shock. Like, this is me? This is all me. I created this.
<v Taylor>我想永远没有什么能比得上看到孩子从你肚子里出来那种超现实的感觉。就像是，哇，我创造了那个生命。你简直惊呆了。就像是，这是我？这一切都是我。我创造了这个。

00:53:58.120 --> 00:54:23.860
<v Taylor>And he came out with his eyes wide open. He was very aware. He's beautiful. He's quiet. He's not a normal baby. He's just a super baby. Black, dark black hair. He's really perfect. If you wanted a kid, he'd be like the blueprint.
<v Taylor>他出来时眼睛睁得大大的。他非常清醒。他很漂亮。他很安静。他不是那种普通的孩子。他简直是个超级宝宝。皮肤黑黑的，头发黑黑的。他真的完美无缺。如果你想要个孩子，他就是蓝图。

00:54:23.860 --> 00:54:38.200
<v Caroline Kitchener>In the end, Taylor wasn't alone in the hospital. She asked her cousin to give her a ride to the hospital. The cousin stayed and took a video of the birth. And Taylor says the cousin must have called other people in the family, because the day she gave birth--
<v Caroline Kitchener>最后，泰勒在医院里并不孤单。她请求表姐开车送她去医院。表姐留下来拍了分娩视频。泰勒说表姐一定打电话给家人了，因为生孩子的当天——

00:54:38.200 --> 00:54:56.270
<v Taylor>My father came, same day. He also showed up the next day. My mom came the next day, and she stayed the night every night after that. And then I also had family that just won't leave. They're like, I won't say or do anything. I just want to be here.
<v Taylor>我父亲来了，同一天。他第二天也出现了。我妈妈第二天也来了，之后每天晚上都留下来。然后还有其他亲戚，怎么也不肯走。他们说：我不会说什么也不会做什么。我只是想待在这里。

00:54:56.270 --> 00:55:18.080
<v Taylor>So I've come down off of my high horse because it's such a huge thing. And I think I underestimated, probably, how huge it was. I had to really humble myself and be OK with people helping me. I'd get, like, two hours a night of sleep. So I just needed help.
<v Taylor>所以我已经从高姿态上下来了，因为这是一件太大的事。我想我低估了它有多重大。我不得不放下身段，接受人们对我的帮助。我一晚只能睡两个小时。所以我真的需要帮助。

00:55:18.080 --> 00:55:19.720
<v Taylor>[BABY FUSSES]
<v Taylor>[婴儿哭闹声]

00:55:19.720 --> 00:55:23.710
<v Taylor>That's him. His pacifier came out.
<v Taylor>那是他。奶嘴掉了。

00:55:23.710 --> 00:55:27.980
<v Taylor>[BABY CRIES]
<v Taylor>[婴儿哭声]

00:55:27.980 --> 00:55:42.780
<v Caroline Kitchener>The baby wakes up while we're talking, Cole Anthony. He's six days old.
<v Caroline Kitchener>在我们谈话时，孩子醒了，科尔·安东尼（Cole Anthony）。他六天大。

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:53.600
<v Caroline Kitchener>For months, I've been thinking about Taylor and wondering about two things. Would she let people in once she had the baby? And also this question.
<v Caroline Kitchener>几个月来，我一直想着泰勒，并在思考两件事。她生下孩子后会接受别人的帮助吗？以及这个问题。

00:55:53.600 --> 00:56:03.910
<v Caroline Kitchener>If these abortion laws were not in effect, if Roe had not been overturned, do you think you would have this baby now?
<v Caroline Kitchener>如果这些堕胎法律没有生效，如果“罗案”没有被推翻，你觉得你现在还会有这个孩子吗？

00:56:03.910 --> 00:56:05.300
<v Taylor>No.
<v Taylor>不会。

00:56:05.300 --> 00:56:10.570
<v Caroline Kitchener>Wow. How does that make you feel?
<v Caroline Kitchener>哇。那让你有什么感觉？

00:56:10.570 --> 00:56:33.600
<v Taylor>It makes me feel bad. It makes me feel bad. But I know for a fact that I've spent so much money on this kid, I've had so much anguish, I've lost relationships. I had to switch jobs. My body has changed. My mind has changed.
<v Taylor>让我感觉很糟。感觉很糟。但我很清楚，为了这个孩子我花了很多钱，经历了这么多痛苦，失去了这么多关系。我不得不换工作。我的身体变了。我的心理也变了。

00:56:33.600 --> 00:57:04.680
<v Taylor>I underestimated what I would have to sacrifice to totally go through with it. And I feel like, although my baby is perfect, just because I have my baby doesn't mean that I'm happy that Roe versus Wade got changed. Because my life is never going to be the same. And I don't like that there's a piece of it that was impacted by a force that wasn't myself.
<v Taylor>我低估了为了完全走下去我必须做出的牺牲。我感觉，虽然我的孩子很完美，但这并不意味着我因为“罗诉韦德案”的改变而感到快乐。因为我的人生再也回不去了。我不喜欢它受影响的那部分是由一个我不受自己掌控的力量所影响的。

00:57:04.680 --> 00:57:43.270
<v Taylor>Like, if I just decided to do it, have a baby, it'd be one thing. But to know that any part of it was challenged by some outside force, it's so messed up. And although right now everything's happy, giggles, newborn stage, this is my life now-- my whole life, not just 18 years. But this is just-- things are really going to change.
<v Taylor>比如，如果是我自己决定要个孩子，那是一回事。但知道其中的任何一部分是被某种外部力量挑战，这太糟糕了。虽然现在一切都很开心，新生儿阶段，这是我的人生——不仅仅是18年，而是我的整个人生。事情真的要变了。

00:57:43.270 --> 00:58:12.530
<v Caroline Kitchener>There will be some moment, probably soon, when we'll all just get used to the idea that people travel hundreds of miles and cross state lines to get abortions. That'll just seem normal, if it doesn't already. It'll seem normal that people have second-trimester abortions at home, that some people will panic and not know what to do with the fetal tissue, or feel too scared to seek medical attention. At some point in the future, that fear will not be remarkable.
<v Caroline Kitchener>也许很快会有那么一个时刻，我们都会习惯这样一个想法：人们为了堕胎不惜跨越数百英里、跨越州界。那将变得很平常，如果现在还不算平常的话。人们在家进行孕中期堕胎、有些人惊慌失措不知如何处理胎儿组织或因为害怕而不敢寻求医疗护理，这些在未来都会变得很正常。在未来的某个时刻，这种恐惧将不再值得大惊小怪。

00:58:12.530 --> 00:58:52.870
<v Caroline Kitchener>And we will also get used to this notion that, for many people, if you get pregnant in a state where abortion is banned and you do not want a baby, you may still wind up with one. We are rapidly moving into that future, where all of this is normal, which means right now may be the last time we can truly feel how much has changed. For the people who wanted abortions nine months ago, the gap between what was and what is is very clear.
<v Caroline Kitchener>我们也将习惯这样一个观念：对于很多人来说，如果你在一个禁止堕胎的州怀孕了，而你又不想要个孩子，你最终可能还是会有一个。我们正在迅速迈向那个未来，在那里这一切都是正常的，这意味着此时此刻可能是我们最后一次能真正感受到世界发生了多大的变化。对于那些九个月前想要堕胎的人来说，“过去”和“现在”之间的鸿沟非常清晰。

00:58:52.870 --> 00:58:56.050
<v Ira Glass>Caroline Kitchener, she's a reporter at the Washington Post.
<v Ira Glass>卡罗琳·基奇纳，她是《华盛顿邮报》的记者。

00:58:56.050 --> 00:59:38.310
<v Ira Glass>["TWO ROADS" BY VALERIE JUNE]
<v Ira Glass>["两条路" 瓦莱丽·琼]

00:59:38.310 --> 01:00:04.340
<v Ira Glass>Well, today's program was produced by Chana Joffe-Walt and Valerie Kipnis and edited by Laura Starcheski. The people who put our program together today include Jane Ackermann, Elna Baker, Sean Cole, Michal Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Cassie Howley, Miki Meek, Alaa Mostafa, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的制作人是Chana Joffe-Walt和Valerie Kipnis，由Laura Starcheski编辑。今天参与制作的人员包括Jane Ackermann, Elna Baker, Sean Cole, Michal Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Cassie Howley, Miki Meek, Alaa Mostafa, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney 和 Diane Wu。我们的执行主编是Sarah Abdurrahman。高级编辑是David Kestenbaum。执行编辑是Emanuele Berry。

01:00:04.340 --> 01:00:37.090
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Peter Wallsten, Kainaz Amaria, Ali Block, "The Nocturnists" podcast, Kate Cole from The Northwest Abortion Access Fund, Elizabeth Nash, and Robert Marshall. Original music for today's show by Christina Courtin and Matt McGinley. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia. He remembers when the Lord spake unto him-- which, judging from what happened to Abraham and Noah and Lot, does not always usually work out so great. Torey says God was like--
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢Peter Wallsten, Kainaz Amaria, Ali Block，《夜班医生》播客，来自西北堕胎援助基金的Kate Cole，Elizabeth Nash和Robert Marshall。今天节目的原创音乐由Christina Courtin和Matt McGinley创作。《美国生活》由PRX（公共广播交换中心）提供给公共广播电台。一如既往地感谢我们节目的联合创始人Torey Malatia先生。他还记得主与他说话的时刻——不过从亚伯拉罕、诺亚和罗得的经历来看，情况并不总是有那么好。Torey说，神当时问——

01:00:37.090 --> 01:00:40.050
<v Lilly>Hey what you doing? And I was like, oh, dear god.
<v Lilly>嘿，你在干嘛？我说，噢，天哪。

01:00:40.050 --> 01:00:44.790
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。

01:00:44.790 --> 01:00:49.790
<v Ira Glass>["TWO ROADS" BY VALERIE JUNE]
